  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nTonight and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening features a negatively-tilted \nshortwave that is gradually opening up as it ejects northeastward \nfrom the Intermountain West towards the Dakotas. Taking a look at \nthe surface pattern, a strong pressure gradient is set up locally \nbetween the surface low to the west and stout high pressure over the \neastern CONUS, helping winds gust during the afternoon at 30-40+ \nmph. Those gusts have slowed down a bit as the nocturnal boundary \nlayer has tried decoupling a bit, but no true inversion seems to be \nable to form, allowing sporadic gusts of 25-35 mph occur through \nmorning. \n\nBy the time that morning rolls around, those southerly winds become \nmore consistent and return to a gusty posture as the surface low \ntakes shape over the WY/MT border region and ejects northeastward \nduring the day. Winds do decrease during the afternoon hours, as the \nmain low-level jet streak shifts east into IA/MN/WI and the low \nmoves farther north from the forecast area. Winds that gust out of \nthe south-southwest at 30-40 mph and decreasing relative humidity \nvalues are raising some concern for extreme fire behavior tomorrow --\n particularly for Knox County where it's pretty dry. Fortunately the \nvolatility of the fire conditions is curbed by decreasing wind \nspeeds as relative humidity values tank in the 20-25% range. A \nRed Flag Warning is in place for Knox County from 12 PM tomorrow\nto 6 PM. Highs top out in the 60s, being the warmest across \nnortheast Nebraska while the effects of the passing system fades\npretty quickly as it ejects away through the evening. \n\nThursday and Beyond:\n\nTemperatures remain warm across the area Thursday, for what will be \na wonderful fall day without the strong winds from the previous two \ndays. Dry weather and only a few clouds will be in place, make this \nthe day you wrap up yard work before the weather turns later in the \nweek! By Friday, we see the narrow ridge from Thursday turn to swift \nsouthwesterly flow aloft in anticipation of a longwave mid/upper \ntrough progressing through the western CONUS. Strong winds whip up \nonce again during the day Friday, with this round of gusts being our \nbest chance of hitting 45-50 mph or more according to the latest EPS \ndata. By the later evening hours, the main front will arrive to east-\ncentral Nebraska, where the forcing from throughout the vertical \ncolumn gradually builds clouds and eventual rainfall across \nnortheast Nebraska (our closest locations to the surface low to the \nnorth) that gradually expands to the southwest. Light precipitation \nshould be the expectation early Saturday as the front slowly edges \neastward, with precipitation increasing above light rain Saturday \nevening into the overnight hours as focused lift maximizes with the \npassage of a local vorticity maximum. Temperatures will only \nfall as the cooler air fills in behind the frontal passage, so \ndressing a bit warmer than you think for afternoon activities \nwill be the way to go if you're outside.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, we're only topping out in the 40s as the \nmid/upper flow becomes zonal and we dry out in the wake of the \ndeparting system. Temperatures only decrease going into the work \nweek for Monday/Tuesday, with highs currently hanging around the \nupper 30s and low 40s for those hauling a turkey home from the \nstore. Towards the middle and end of next week, we're looking active \nweather-wise, but the sheer amount of spread will keep us from being \ntoo declarative about any home run precipitation chances. EPS and \nGEFS members have some aspect of snow to them, but the likelihood of \nany impactful to that would disrupt Thanksgiving travel locally \nappears to be low. Check with the forecast as we get closer, if \nyou're traveling regionally as a larger weather-maker will be \nin the region, with the chances for flakes and ice increasing as\nyou move north and west.",
